rse in which both are attested. Roots occurring more than 700 times across the whole corpus are excluded as counting partners (treated as function-word-like given their extreme frequency); the top 12 remaining partners are kept, ranked by co-occurrence count. Co-occurrence describes distribution in the text. It does not by itself establish meaning.","_pmiMethod":"Pointwise mutual information over the same verse-level attestation events: PMI(r1,r2) = log2( count(r1,r2) × 6236 / (verseCount(r1) × verseCount(r2)) ), base 2, rounded to 2 decimals. 6236 is every verse in the corpus (22 carry no root-annotated token at all and legitimately count toward every root's \"absent\" side). Same function-word exclusion as coRoots; a pair needs at least 3 shared verses to be ranked, since below that a single shared verse between two rare roots produces an enormous but meaningless score. This ranks by distinctiveness, not frequency — it is a different question from coRoots, not a more-correct version of it; both are kept.","_pmiTopN":10,"_pmiMinCooccurrence":3,"_computed":"2026-07-28"}